 \subsection{A leakage-safe sequence model}
 We build one sequence per student in time order. At step $i$ the model sees the
 features of interaction $i$ and predicts interaction $i\!+\!1$. Because the prediction
 for a target comes from the LSTM state after reading only the steps before it, a
 target's own features never reach the model that predicts it. The backbone is a plain
-single-layer LSTM (hidden size $64$, dropout $0.4$) with two heads on the next-step
+single-layer LSTM (hidden size $64$, output dropout $0.4$) with two heads on the next-step
 state. The correctness head is one output trained with cross-entropy. The deficiency
-head has $55$ outputs, but since a missing concept is always one of the associated
-ones, we push the outputs for the rest down to a very large negative value before the
+head has $55$ outputs, but we restrict predictions to the associated
+concepts and push the outputs for the rest down to a very large negative value before the
 loss and the prediction. This drops about $53$ of $55$ obvious negatives per example
 and lets the model focus on the real candidates. Those remaining outputs are trained
 with a focal version of cross-entropy \cite{lin2017focal}, which lowers the weight of
 the many easy negatives. The model is kept small on purpose, because with six students
-anything bigger just memorizes.
+anything bigger risks memorization; larger models were not tested.
+
+For completed step $i$, $h_i=\mathrm{LSTM}(x_i,h_{i-1})$ feeds a correctness
+head $u_i$ and deficiency head $z_{i,c}$. Let $a_{i+1,c}$ be the next question's
+associated-concept mask and $y_{i+1,c}$ its gold deficiency label. The masking and
+loss over a student's training targets $I$ are
+\begin{align}
+\tilde z_{i,c}&=\begin{cases}z_{i,c}&a_{i+1,c}=1,\\-10^9&a_{i+1,c}=0,\end{cases}
+\quad p_{i,c}=\sigma(\tilde z_{i,c}),\quad
+p^*_{i,c}=y_{i+1,c}p_{i,c}+(1-y_{i+1,c})(1-p_{i,c}),\label{eq:mask}\\
+L&=\operatorname{BCE}(u,r)+
+\frac{\sum_{i\in I,c}a_{i+1,c}\max(1-p^*_{i,c},10^{-6})^2\operatorname{BCE}(\tilde z_{i,c},y_{i+1,c})}
+{\sum_{i\in I,c}a_{i+1,c}}.\label{eq:loss}
+\end{align}
+BCE uses logits; correctness BCE is averaged over $I$. There are no additional
+class weights. Empty masks contribute zero; each student gives one optimizer step.
+
+Six source records have missing labels outside this mask: four training, one validation
+and one test record. The loss ignores these positives; evaluation keeps them. Thus
+58 of 59 test concept positives are reachable. We retain this constrained task and
+original labels; expanding masks from target labels would leak information. Excluding
+the one inconsistent test row gives F1@13 A/B/C = 24.28/27.39/32.07\%, with no
+refitting. Full exceptions and diagnostics are in \code{report/Diagnostic-Supplement.md}.
